Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 05:26:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] TOG-687: stop shipping an ACP-fatal `effort` default on the
 Claude lane

The claude_local adapter's default cheap profile declared `effort: "low"`.
`resolveModelProfileApplication` spreads the adapter default FIRST and the
agent's stored profile second, so that value is re-supplied to any cheap
profile that does not explicitly pin `effort` to a falsy value. TOG-685
closed this at the fleet level by writing `effort: ""` onto all 47 agent
rows; that holds only for rows that exist today. Enabling a cheap profile
later -- console, API, or plugin -- silently re-arms it. Removing the key
from the adapter default is the source-side fix, and the merge order is
exactly why deleting it from a stored profile was never enough.

The exposure is wider than the cheap profile, which is the second change
here. `effort` is a documented top-level claude_local config field, and
the ACP lane pushes it at set_config_option for every agent except codex.
The Claude ACP backend advertises the `effort` option CONDITIONALLY, on
whether the selected model supports it (buildConfigOptions in
claude-agent-acp), so acpx raises ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL and the
run dies in configure_session. Any claude_local ACP agent with a
top-level `effort` hits this with no cheap profile involved -- measured,
not inferred: a probe against the real executor returned
configOptions [{"key":"effort","value":"low"}].

An unsupported *preference* should not kill a run, so a rejected optional
option now degrades with a stderr line saying it did not apply. `model`
is deliberately NOT in that set: continuing after a rejected model would
run the turn on a different model, at a different price, while reporting
success. Matching is on the error's `code`, not its message text, so an
upstream wording change cannot quietly make a fatal path degradable.

Verified on the live tree with mutation controls -- stock sources plus
these tests fail (adapter default re-supplies `effort: "low"`; the
rejected-effort run exits 1), patched they pass. 157 acpx-engine tests
and the claude-local package suite are unchanged; the one failing test in
that package (acp.test.ts "reports ACP prerequisites") fails identically
on stock sources and is unrelated.
